1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
Mortgage rates just went back above seven percent. And here's the weird part: the Fed didn't do it.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:14,000
So why is your mortgage getting more expensive? It comes down to one number most people never look at.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
That number is the ten-year Treasury yield. Think of it as the interest rate the government pays to borrow money for ten years.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Banks look at that rate and say, okay, if lending to the government pays this much, a home loan has to pay more. That's the whole trick.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,000
So when the ten-year yield goes up, mortgage rates follow it. Not the Fed. The bond market.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Here's what this means for your wallet. If you already have a fixed-rate mortgage, nothing changes. Your payment is locked.

7
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:58,500
But if you're buying a house right now, the same house costs you hundreds of dollars more every single month than it did a year ago.

8
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:07,000
And that's not because the house got nicer. It's because the price of borrowing money went up.

9
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Now why did yields go up in the first place? Three reasons. Oil, inflation, and how much the government is borrowing.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:25,000
When oil gets expensive, almost everything gets more expensive a little later. Gas, shipping, food. That's inflation.

11
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:34,000
And when inflation is high, people who lend money want a higher interest rate to make up for it. That pushes yields up.

12
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:43,000
What about your job? Higher borrowing costs make it more expensive for companies to grow, so hiring usually slows down.

13
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Your groceries? Those follow oil and shipping costs. Your 401k? Most of it tracks the S and P 500, and stocks do not love higher rates.

14
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Here's what to watch next. The Fed meets in December, and the next inflation report comes out before that.

15
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:10,000
This is education, not investment advice. If this helped, subscribe, and tell me what you want explained next.
